          "type": "singleCorrect",
          "level": 1,
          "question": {
            "text": "Let $\\mathrm{T}(k)$ be the statement $1+3+5+\\ldots+$ $(2 k-1)=k^{2}+10$\r\nWhich of the following is correct?",
            "image": null
          },
          "options": [
            {
              "id": "lVaLE",
              "text": "$\\mathrm{T}(1)$ is true",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "kQj4O",
              "text": "$\\mathrm{T}(k)$ is true $\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{T}(k+1)$ is true",
              "isCorrect": true,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "nohjK",
              "text": "$\\mathrm{T}(n)$ is true for all $n \\in \\mathbf{N}$",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            },
            {
              "id": "WOo3X",
              "text": "All above are correct",
              "isCorrect": false,
              "image": null
            }
          ],
          "correctValue": null,
          "solution": {
            "text": "When $\\mathrm{k}=1, \\mathrm{LHS}=1$ but $\\mathrm{RHS}=1+10=11$\r\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{T}(1)$ is not true Let $\\mathrm{T}(k)$ is true. That is\r\n$\\begin{array}{l}\r\n1+3+5+\\ldots .+(2 k-1)=k^{2}+10 \\\\\r\n\\text { Now, } 1+3+5+\\ldots . .+(2 k-1)+(2 k+1) \\\\\r\n=k^{2}+10+2 k+1=(k+1)^{2}+10\r\n\\end{array}$\r\n$\\therefore \\mathrm{T}(k+1)$ is true.\r\nThat is $\\mathrm{T}(k)$ is true $\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{T}(k+1)$ is true.\r\nBut $\\mathrm{T}(n)$ is not true for all $n \\in \\mathbf{N},$ as $\\mathrm{T}(1)$ is not true.",
            "image": null
          },
